204 0 obj
<<
/Pg 28 0 R
/P 1867 0 R
/A 1868 0 R
/Alt (Table showing vulnerabilities, associated risks, and resilience considerations, across 5 themes. These themes are operational resilience, global equity and credit markets, sovereign debt markets, households and businesses, and system liquidity. Operational resilience is a domestic and global theme. Vulnerabilities cited: more complex and interconnected financial system, and reliance on common material service providers. Associated risks cited: cyber-attack or operational outage disrupts critical services, disruption to payments and other financial market infrastructures, and contagion through common service providers and critical infrastructure. Resilience considerations cited: governance, risk culture and risk management practices, regulatory coordination arrangements, information sharing across financial institutions, regulators, and security authorities \(including business continuity plans\). Global equity and credit markets is a global theme. Vulnerabilities cited: compressed risk premia and elevated prices, concentrated and opaque exposures to the AI investment cycle, material participation in markets by leveraged non-bank financial institutions. Associated risks cited: sharp and disorderly price adjustments, forced deleveraging and asset fire sales, spillovers across equity and funding markets. Resilience considerations cited: liquidity preparedness, enhanced data collection, capital to absorb losses. Sovereign debt markets is a global theme. Vulnerabilities cited: high and rising government debt, greater reliance on leveraged/price-sensitive investors. Associated risks cited: shifts in investor confidence and sharp repricing, forced deleveraging by leveraged investors, and spillovers to Australian funding markets. Resilience considerations cited: Liquidity preparedness, and central bank liquidity facilities. Households and businesses is a domestic theme. Vulnerabilities cited: high household leverage and asset prices. Associated risks cited: labour market shock, challenges to debt serviceability, and extended housing market downturn. Resilience considerations cited: sound lending standards, household equity and liquidity buffers, and bank capital and loan loss provisioning. System liquidity is a domestic theme. Vulnerabilities cited: concentrated holdings of liquid assets in markets that may not perform well in stress, and growing role of the superannuation industry in the Australian financial system. Associated risks cited: unexpected liquidity calls on superannuation funds, and inability to monetise bank debt and semi-government securities in stress. Resilience considerations cited: Liquidity preparedness and liquidity risk management practices, superannuation funds are currently in the accumulation phase, and central bank liquidity facilities.)
/K 3
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
>>
endobj
