/Alt (A line chart showing government net interest payments as a share of GDP for the United States, United Kingdom, Euro Area, Australia and Japan. The series combine historical outcomes and OECD forecasts, with dots indicating forecast observations from 2026 onwards. The euro area aggregate covers Austria, Belgium, Cyprus, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Greece, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, Malta, Netherlands, Portugal, Slovakia, Slovenia and Spain. The chart highlights the increase in debt servicing burdens across advanced economies following the sharp rise in policy interest rates in recent years. Net interest payments are highest in the United States, where large fiscal deficits and elevated government debt levels have increased borrowing costs. Interest burdens have also risen in the United Kingdom and euro area, while remaining comparatively lower in Australia and Japan. OECD forecasts suggest that net interest payments will remain elevated or continue to increase over the next few years, implying ongoing pressure on public finances as governments refinance debt at higher interest rates.)
